IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ea-part2`
IMPORT `ea-part2-continuity`
IMPORT `ea-part3-salary`
IMPORT `ea-part4-hours`
IMPORT `ea-parts5to8`
IMPORT `ea-part9-maternity`
IMPORT `ea-part10-leave`
IMPORT `ea-part12-records`
IMPORT `ea-part12-retrenchment`
IMPORT `ea-retrenchment-reporting-notification`

-- Employment Act 1968 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal
-- consolidation in force from 5 December 2025 -- THE EMPLOYER'S SEAT LAYER.
--
-- SEAT: the employer -- an individual or a company that employs someone under
-- a contract of service, asking about one employment (or, for retrenchment
-- reporting, one exercise of giving notices).
--
-- This is the SECOND seat on this encoding, beside the employee's (ea-seat.l4),
-- under the ruling of 21 October 2026 on more than one seat. It phrases the
-- rules already encoded in the modules imported above as the questions an
-- employer asks: whom the Act covers, how much notice I must give or pay, when
-- I may dismiss or suspend, when I must pay and what I may deduct, the rest
-- days, overtime and leave I must give, maternity pay, the records and terms I
-- must give, retrenchment reporting, and what I can be penalised for.
--
-- Every question calls an existing rule with the same parameters and CHANGES
-- NONE OF THEM; nothing here is new law. Both seat layers draw on the same
-- rules, so where they ask about the same facts they cannot disagree; the
-- mirror cases in ea-cases-seat-employer.l4 show it.
--
-- NAMES. Every name defined here begins "As the employer, " so that no name can
-- clash with ea-seat.l4's (which are the employee's "Am I ...", "Can my
-- employer ..."). The two layers import together without "multiple
-- definitions".
--
-- Each question is published with @export so that it can be deployed and asked
-- from a form. Where the Act's answer turns on a judgment it hands to someone
-- else (the due inquiry, a reasonable excuse, the Commissioner's permission),
-- the existing modules take it as a fact, and so does this layer; see NOTES.md.
--
-- Questions an employer would ask that the modules do not decide are listed in
-- NOTES.md under "Seats", not answered here.

§ `Employment Act 1968 -- the employer's questions`

§§ `Whom the Act covers, and which Parts apply`

-- s 2(1). "Employee": a person who has entered into or works under a contract
-- of service, but not a seafarer, a domestic worker, or a class the Minister
-- has declared not to be employees.
@export Is the person I employ an "employee" under the Act (s 2(1))?
GIVEN `works under a contract of service` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person has entered into or works under a contract of service with you
      `is a seafarer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person is a seafarer
      `is a domestic worker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person is a domestic worker
      `is in a class the Minister has declared not to be employees` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person is in a class the Minister has declared by Gazette notification not to be employees
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, is this person an employee under the Act` IF
    `is an employee for the purposes of the Act` `works under a contract of service` `is a seafarer` `is a domestic worker` `is in a class the Minister has declared not to be employees`

-- s 35 (the same words as s 33(1)). Part 4 -- rest days, hours of work,
-- overtime -- covers a "workman" (an employee doing mainly manual labour)
-- earning up to $4,500 a month, and any other employee, not a manager or
-- executive, earning up to $2,600 a month, basic salary only.
@export Must I apply the Part 4 rest day, hours and overtime rules to this employee (s 35)?
GIVEN `the worker` IS A Worker @desc Whether the employee is a workman (mainly manual work) or a manager or executive, and their basic monthly salary in dollars
      `the caps` IS A SalaryCaps @desc The salary thresholds in force (the Act's own: $4,500 for a workman, $2,600 for others)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, must I apply Part 4 to this employee` IF
    `this Part applies to the employee` `the worker` `the caps`

-- s 68(1)-(4). A "child" is a person under 15. A child must not be employed in
-- an industrial or non-industrial undertaking, except in a family-only
-- industrial undertaking, or in light work suited to the child's capacity in a
-- non-industrial undertaking from age 13 (a medical officer's certificate is
-- conclusive on suitability).
@export Would employing this child break the Act (s 68)?
GIVEN `the worker` IS A YoungWorker @desc The child's age, the kind of undertaking, whether it employs only family, and whether the work is light work suited to the child (or so certified by a medical officer)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, would employing this child break the Act` IF
    `employing this child contravenes section 68(1)` `the worker`

§§ `Notice, and ending the contract`

-- ss 8, 10(2), 10(3). The notice is the same for both sides (s 10(2)): the
-- contract's figure, unless shorter than the Act's minimum for the length of
-- service (1 day under 26 weeks, 1 week to 2 years, 2 weeks to 5 years, 4
-- weeks from 5 years), in which case s 8 voids it and the minimum applies.
@export How many days of notice must I give to end this employee's contract (ss 8, 10(2), 10(3))?
GIVEN `the terms` IS A ContractTerms @desc What the contract of service says about notice
      `the service` IS A ServiceLength @desc How long the employee has worked for you
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `As the employer, how many days of notice must I give` IS
    `the notice period that governs, in days` `the terms` `the service`

-- s 11(1). "Salary in lieu of notice": either party may end the contract
-- without notice by paying the salary at the gross rate of pay (before
-- deductions) that would have accrued over the notice period.
@export How much must I pay in lieu of notice, in dollars (s 11(1))?
GIVEN `the gross daily rate of pay` IS A NUMBER @desc The employee's gross rate of pay for one day, before deductions, in dollars
      `the terms` IS A ContractTerms @desc What the contract of service says about notice
      `the service` IS A ServiceLength @desc How long the employee has worked for you
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `As the employer, how much must I pay in lieu of notice` IS
    `the payment in lieu of notice` `the gross daily rate of pay` `the terms` `the service`

-- s 10(4), (5). The notice given is long enough if it is at least the period
-- that governs (the day of giving counts), or the other side waived notice.
@export Is the notice I have given long enough (s 10)?
GIVEN `the notice` IS A Notice @desc The notice you gave: whether written, how many days (counting the day it was given), and whether the employee waived notice
      `the terms` IS A ContractTerms @desc What the contract of service says about notice
      `the service` IS A ServiceLength @desc How long the employee has worked for you
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, is the notice I have given long enough` IF
    `the notice is long enough` `the notice` `the terms` `the service`

-- s 11(2). Either party may end the contract without notice, and without
-- payment in lieu, on a "wilful breach" by the other party of a condition of
-- the contract.
@export May I end the contract without notice or payment because the employee wilfully broke a condition of it (s 11(2))?
GIVEN `there was a wilful breach by the other party of a condition of the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the employee wilfully broke a condition of the contract of service
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, may I end the contract without notice for the employee's wilful breach` IF
    `the party may terminate without notice and without payment under section 11(2)` `there was a wilful breach by the other party of a condition of the contract`

-- s 13(2). The employee is "deemed to have broken the contract" by being
-- absent for more than 2 days in a row without prior leave, with no
-- reasonable excuse or without telling (or trying to tell) the employer.
@export Is my employee treated as having broken the contract by being absent from work (s 13(2))?
GIVEN `the absence` IS AN Absence @desc The absence: how many days in a row, whether you had given leave, whether the employee had a reasonable excuse, and whether they told or tried to tell you
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, is my employee treated as having broken the contract by being absent` IF
    `the employee is deemed to have broken the contract` `the absence`

-- s 13(1). The employer is deemed to have broken the contract if it fails to
-- pay salary in accordance with Part 3.
@export Am I treated as having broken the contract by failing to pay salary as Part 3 requires (s 13(1))?
GIVEN `the employer failed to pay salary in accordance with Part 3` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you failed to pay salary as Part 3 requires (on time, in the right manner, without unauthorised deductions)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, am I treated as having broken the contract by not paying salary` IF
    `the employer is deemed to have broken the contract` `the employer failed to pay salary in accordance with Part 3`

-- s 15. The employee may leave without notice if they or their dependant are
-- immediately threatened with danger to the person by violence or disease that
-- the contract did not undertake.
@export May my employee leave without notice because of danger (s 15)?
GIVEN `the facts` IS A DangerFacts @desc The danger the employee or their dependant faces, and whether the contract undertook that risk
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, may my employee leave without notice because of danger` IF
    `the employee may terminate without notice under section 15` `the facts`

§§ `Dismissal and suspension`

-- s 14(1). After "due inquiry" (a proper inquiry into the alleged misconduct)
-- the employer may dismiss without notice for misconduct inconsistent with the
-- express or implied conditions of service.
@export May I dismiss this employee without notice for misconduct (s 14(1))?
GIVEN `the facts` IS A DismissalFacts @desc The misconduct, and whether you held a due inquiry
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, may I dismiss without notice for misconduct` IF
    `the employer may dismiss without notice under section 14(1)` `the facts`

-- s 14(1)(b). Instead of dismissing, after the same due inquiry, the employer
-- may suspend without salary for no more than one week.
@export May I suspend this employee without pay (s 14(1)(b))?
GIVEN `the facts` IS A DismissalFacts @desc The misconduct, the inquiry, and how many days the suspension lasts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, may I suspend without pay` IF
    `the suspension is lawful under section 14(1)(b)` `the facts`

-- s 14(2A). Who may claim against the employer for wrongful dismissal: any
-- employee not a manager or executive; a manager or executive dismissed
-- without notice and without salary in lieu; or one dismissed with notice (or
-- salary in lieu) after 6 months' service.
@export Can the employee I dismissed claim against me for wrongful dismissal (s 14(2A))?
GIVEN `the employee` IS A Dismissed @desc The employee's position, how you dismissed them, and how long they served you
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, can the employee I dismissed claim for wrongful dismissal` IF
    `the employee may lodge a wrongful dismissal claim` `the employee`

-- ss 18, 18A. A change of employer -- a statutory substitution, the employer's
-- death, a change of partners or trustees, or the transfer of an undertaking
-- -- does not break the continuity of the employee's service, which counts for
-- notice and for the benefit qualifying periods.
@export Does the change of employer keep the employee's period of service continuous (ss 18, 18A)?
GIVEN `the change` IS AN EmployerChange @desc How the employer changed (substitution by law, death, change of partners or trustees, or transfer of the undertaking the employee worked in)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, is the employee's service continuous across the change of employer` IF
    `the continuity of the period of employment is preserved` `the change`

§§ `Paying salary`

-- ss 21 to 23. The time I have to pay: 7 days after the salary period for
-- ordinary salary, 14 for overtime, 3 days after a dismissal, on the day the
-- contract ends (on completion, or when the employee leaves with due notice),
-- and 7 days where the employee leaves without notice.
@export How many days do I have to pay this salary (ss 21 to 23)?
GIVEN `the occasion` IS A PaymentOccasion @desc What the payment is for
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `As the employer, how many days do I have to pay` IS
    `the number of days allowed for payment` `the occasion`

@export Did I pay late (ss 21 to 23)?
GIVEN `the occasion` IS A PaymentOccasion @desc What the payment was for
      `the days taken to pay` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days after the end of the salary period (or after the dismissal or termination) you paid
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, did I pay late` IF
    `the payment was late` `the occasion` `the days taken to pay`

-- s 20A. For a monthly-rated employee who did not work the whole month
-- (started late, left early, unpaid leave, national service): monthly gross
-- rate divided by the days required to work, times the days worked, a day of
-- 5 hours or less counting as half.
@export How much salary must I pay for an incomplete month, in dollars (s 20A)?
GIVEN `the month` IS AN IncompleteMonth @desc The employee's monthly gross rate of pay, the days they were required to work that month and the days they actually worked
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `As the employer, how much salary must I pay for an incomplete month` IS
    `the salary due for the incomplete month` `the month`

§§ `Deductions from salary`

-- ss 26, 27. No deduction unless the Act authorises it (or a court order or tax
-- recovery requires it). Deductions for accommodation, amenities and services,
-- cooperative society payments and any other deduction need the employee's
-- written consent, which the employee may withdraw before it is made.
@export May I make this deduction from the employee's salary (ss 26, 27)?
GIVEN `the kind` IS A DeductionKind @desc What the deduction is for
      `the consent` IS A Consent @desc Whether the employee gave written consent, and whether they withdrew it in writing before the deduction
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, may I make this deduction` IF
    `the deduction may lawfully be made` `the kind` `the consent`

-- s 29. For damage or loss through the employee's neglect or default: no more
-- than the loss, no more than a quarter of one month's wages unless the
-- Commissioner for Labour permits more, and only after the employee has had a
-- chance to "show cause" (explain why it should not be made).
@export May I deduct this amount for damage or loss the employee caused (s 29)?
GIVEN `the deduction` IS A DamagesDeduction @desc The loss, the employee's month's wages, the amount you propose, any permission from the Commissioner, and whether the employee was given a chance to show cause
      `the proportions` IS A Proportions @desc The deduction limits in force (the Act's own: one quarter, one quarter, one quarter, and 50%)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, may I deduct this amount for damage or loss` IF
    `the damages deduction may be made` `the deduction` `the proportions`

-- s 28(2). A deduction for absence may not be a larger share of the salary for
-- the period than the time absent is of the time the employee was required to
-- work.
@export What is the most I may deduct for the employee's absence, in dollars (s 28(2))?
GIVEN `the salary for the period at the gross rate` IS A NUMBER @desc The salary for the salary period at the gross rate of pay, in dollars
      `the period for which the employee was absent` IS A NUMBER @desc How long the employee was absent (days or hours)
      `the total period the employee was required to work` IS A NUMBER @desc How long the employee was required to work in the salary period (same unit)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `As the employer, what is the most I may deduct for absence` IS
    `the maximum deduction for absence` `the salary for the period at the gross rate` `the period for which the employee was absent` `the total period the employee was required to work`

-- s 30(2). Deductions for accommodation, amenities and services: no more than
-- the value supplied, and in total no more than a quarter of the salary for
-- the salary period.
@export Are my deductions for accommodation, amenities and services within the limits (s 30)?
GIVEN `the total of such deductions in the period` IS A NUMBER @desc The total of those deductions in the salary period, in dollars
      `the value of what was supplied` IS A NUMBER @desc The value of the accommodation, amenity or service supplied, in dollars
      `the salary payable for the period` IS A NUMBER @desc The salary payable for the salary period, in dollars
      `the proportions` IS A Proportions @desc The deduction limits in force (the Act's own: one quarter, one quarter, one quarter, and 50%)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, are my accommodation deductions within the limits` IF
    `the accommodation deductions are within section 30` `the total of such deductions in the period` `the value of what was supplied` `the salary payable for the period` `the proportions`

-- s 32. All deductions in a salary period, except for absence, recovery of
-- advances, loans and overpayments, and cooperative society payments, may not
-- exceed 50% of the salary for the period -- unless it is the last salary on
-- termination or completion.
@export Are my deductions this salary period within the 50% cap (s 32)?
GIVEN `the total of countable deductions in the period` IS A NUMBER @desc The total of deductions this salary period, leaving out those for absence, recovering advances, loans or overpayments, and cooperative society payments, in dollars
      `the salary payable for the period` IS A NUMBER @desc The salary payable for the salary period, in dollars
      `this is the last salary on termination or completion` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this is the last salary, on termination or completion of the contract
      `the proportions` IS A Proportions @desc The deduction limits in force (the Act's own: one quarter, one quarter, one quarter, and 50%)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, are my deductions within the 50% cap` IF
    `the section 32 cap is satisfied` `the total of countable deductions in the period` `the salary payable for the period` `this is the last salary on termination or completion` `the proportions`

§§ `Rest days, hours and overtime`

-- s 36. Each week, one whole rest day without pay (Sunday or a day the
-- employer fixes), or for a shift worker a continuous 30 hours instead.
@export Have I allowed this employee a rest day this week (s 36)?
GIVEN `the arrangement` IS A RestDayArrangement @desc Whether a whole rest day was allowed, or for a shift worker a continuous 30 hours, and how the rest day is fixed
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, have I allowed a rest day this week` IF
    `a rest day was allowed in the week` `the arrangement`

-- s 37. What I must pay for work on the employee's rest day: half a day's, one
-- day's, or more pay depending on the hours, doubled where the work was at my
-- request, with hours beyond normal hours at one and a half times.
@export What must I pay for the employee's work on a rest day, in dollars (s 37)?
GIVEN `the work` IS A RestDayWork @desc The employee's normal hours, the hours worked on the rest day, their rates of pay, and whose request it was
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `As the employer, what must I pay for rest day work` IS
    `the payment for working on the rest day` `the work`

-- s 38(4). Overtime at not less than one and a half times the "hourly basic
-- rate of pay" (basic pay, without allowances, per hour).
@export How much overtime must I pay, in dollars (s 38(4))?
GIVEN `the extra hours` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours of overtime the employee worked
      `the hourly basic rate of pay` IS A NUMBER @desc The employee's basic pay per hour, without allowances, in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `As the employer, how much overtime must I pay` IS
    `the overtime payment` `the extra hours` `the hourly basic rate of pay`

-- s 38(1), (4). The hours in a day beyond the daily limit (8, or 9 where the
-- contract has shorter days elsewhere in the week or a 5-day week) are extra
-- work that attracts overtime.
@export How many of the employee's hours today must I pay as overtime (s 38(1), (4))?
GIVEN `the hours` IS A HoursWorked @desc The hours the employee worked and how their working week is arranged
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `As the employer, how many hours today attract overtime` IS
    `the daily hours attracting overtime` `the hours`

-- s 38(1)(a). No more than 6 consecutive hours without a period of leisure.
@export Have I made the employee work more than 6 hours in a row without a break (s 38(1)(a))?
GIVEN `the hours` IS A HoursWorked @desc The hours the employee worked, including the longest run without a break
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, have I broken the 6 consecutive hours rule` IF
    `section 38(1)(a) was contravened` `the hours`

-- s 38(5). No more than 72 hours of overtime a month.
@export Have I let the employee work more than 72 hours of overtime this month (s 38(5))?
GIVEN `the hours` IS A HoursWorked @desc The hours the employee worked, including overtime this month
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, have I broken the 72 hour overtime limit` IF
    `section 38(5) was contravened` `the hours`

§§ `Holidays and leave`

-- s 88. A paid holiday at the gross rate of pay on each public holiday during
-- the employment -- unless another day was substituted by agreement, the
-- employee is on unpaid leave at their own request, or the holiday fell on a
-- non-working day and I gave a day off instead.
@export Must I give the employee a paid holiday for this public holiday (s 88)?
GIVEN `the facts` IS A PublicHolidayFacts @desc The public holiday, and any substituted day, rest day, non-working day or unpaid leave
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, must I give a paid holiday` IF
    `the employee is entitled to a paid holiday` `the facts`

-- s 88A. Paid "annual leave": 7 days in the first year of service, one more
-- day for each further year to 14; pro-rated in a part year; nothing under 3
-- months, and none where the employee was absent without permission or excuse
-- on more than 20% of working days.
@export How many days of paid annual leave must I give this employee this year (s 88A)?
GIVEN `the service` IS A LeaveService @desc The employee's months and years of service, the months served this year, and any unexcused absence on more than 20% of working days
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `As the employer, how many days of annual leave must I give` IS
    `the annual leave days the employee is entitled to` `the service`

-- s 89. Paid sick leave after examination by a medical practitioner: up to 14
-- days of outpatient sick leave a year, or up to 60 where hospitalisation is
-- necessary, scaled down in the first 6 months.
@export How many days of paid sick leave must I allow this employee this year (s 89)?
GIVEN `the facts` IS A SickLeaveFacts @desc The employee's months of service, whether a doctor examined them, and any hospitalisation
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `As the employer, how many days of paid sick leave must I allow` IS
    `the paid sick leave ceiling in days` `the facts`

-- s 45. No retrenchment benefit is owed to an employee with less than 2 years'
-- continuous service dismissed for redundancy or reorganisation.
@export Does the Act bar any retrenchment benefit because the employee's service was too short (s 45)?
GIVEN `the service` IS A ServiceForBenefits @desc The employee's years of continuous service, and why you dismissed them
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, does short service bar a retrenchment benefit` IF
    `section 45 bars any retrenchment benefit` `the service`

§§ `Maternity`

-- s 76(1A), (2B), s 83. I must pay the "benefit period" -- 8 weeks of the
-- maternity absence -- if she has served me at least 3 months before
-- confinement (giving birth) and has not worked for another employer during
-- the absence.
@export Must I pay this employee during her maternity benefit period (ss 76, 83)?
GIVEN `the facts` IS A MaternityFacts @desc How she takes her maternity absence, the delivery date, her service with you, her daily pay, and whether she worked for another employer during it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, must I pay during the maternity benefit period` IF
    `she is entitled to payment during the benefit period` `the facts`

-- s 76(1A). The gross rate of pay for each day of the 8-week benefit period.
@export How much maternity pay must I pay, in dollars (s 76(1A))?
GIVEN `the facts` IS A MaternityFacts @desc How she takes her maternity absence, the delivery date, her service with you, her daily pay, and whether she worked for another employer during it
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `As the employer, how much maternity pay must I pay` IS
    `the payment for the benefit period` `the facts`

-- s 81. While she is absent on maternity leave, I may not give notice of
-- dismissal during the absence, or on a day such that it would expire during
-- it.
@export Would my notice of dismissal around her maternity absence be unlawful (s 81)?
GIVEN `the employee is absent in accordance with this Part` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether she is absent on maternity leave under Part 9
      `notice of dismissal was given during the absence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the notice of dismissal is given during that absence
      `the notice would expire during the absence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the notice would run out during that absence
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, would my notice of dismissal during her maternity absence be unlawful` IF
    `the notice of dismissal is unlawful under section 81` `the employee is absent in accordance with this Part` `notice of dismissal was given during the absence` `the notice would expire during the absence`

-- s 82. Knowingly employing her in the 4 weeks immediately after her
-- confinement is an offence.
@export Am I guilty of an offence for employing her in the 4 weeks after she gave birth (s 82)?
GIVEN `the employer employed her during the 4 weeks immediately following confinement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you employed her at any time in the 4 weeks immediately after her confinement
      `the employer knew` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you knew you were doing so
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, am I guilty of the section 82 offence` IF
    `the employer is guilty of an offence under section 82` `the employer employed her during the 4 weeks immediately following confinement` `the employer knew`

§§ `Records, key employment terms and pay slips`

-- s 95(1), (3). I must make and keep, for the prescribed period, employee
-- records with the prescribed particulars for every employee and former
-- employee; an incomplete or inaccurate record is a failure whether or not I
-- knew.
@export Have I kept this employee's record as the Act requires (s 95(1), (3))?
GIVEN `the record` IS AN EmployeeRecord @desc Whether you made the record, whether it has the prescribed particulars, whether you kept it for the prescribed period, and whether it is incomplete or inaccurate
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, have I kept the employee record as required` IF
    `the employer has complied with section 95(1)` `the record`

-- s 95A. "Key employment terms": the written record of the main terms of the
-- job, due within 14 days of the start of employment, for contracts made on or
-- after 1 April 2016 and service of at least the prescribed minimum.
@export Have I failed to give the employee the written key employment terms in time (s 95A)?
GIVEN `the facts` IS A KeyTermsFacts @desc When the contract was made, how long the employee is employed for, and when (if at all) you gave the written key employment terms
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, have I failed to give the key employment terms in time` IF
    `the employer has contravened section 95A` `the facts`

-- s 96. A pay slip (paper or an electronic record the employee can keep) for
-- each salary period and for any sum paid on dismissal or termination, in time,
-- in the prescribed form, complete and accurate -- unless exempted.
@export Have I met the pay slip duty (s 96)?
GIVEN `the facts` IS A PaySlipFacts @desc The pay slips you gave, when, in what form, whether complete and accurate, and any exemption
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, have I met the pay slip duty` IF
    `the employer has complied with section 96` `the facts`

§§ `Retrenchment reporting`

-- s 96A and the Employment (Retrenchment Reporting) Notification 2019, para 3.
-- An employer with 10 or more covered employees on the day it gives a covered
-- employee (open-ended contract, or a term of at least 6 months) notice of
-- "retrenchment" (ending the contract at the employer's initiative for
-- redundancy or reorganisation) must report it to the Commissioner for Labour.
@export Must I report this retrenchment to the Commissioner for Labour (s 96A; Retrenchment Reporting Notification 2019, para 3)?
GIVEN `the notice` IS A `Notice of retrenchment` @desc The notice of retrenchment: the employee's contract, your workforce on the day, the reason, and the dates (day 0 is 1 November 2021)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, must I report this retrenchment` IF
    `a retrenchment report must be given for the notice` `the notice`

-- Notification para 4. The report is due no later than 5 working days (not a
-- Saturday, Sunday or public holiday) after the day the notice is given. Days
-- are day numbers: day 0 is Monday 1 November 2021.
@export What is the last day for my retrenchment report, as a day number (Retrenchment Reporting Notification 2019, para 4)?
GIVEN `the day the notice is given` IS A NUMBER @desc The day you gave the notice of retrenchment, as a day number (day 0 is Monday 1 November 2021)
      `public holidays` IS A LIST OF NUMBER @desc The public holidays in the period, as day numbers
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `As the employer, what is the last day for my retrenchment report` IS
    `the last day for the retrenchment report` `the day the notice is given` `public holidays`

-- s 96A(2) with the Notification. Compliance is counted employee by employee:
-- every notice needing a report must be reported in time, in the form, with
-- every particular.
@export Have I complied with the retrenchment reporting duty (s 96A(2))?
GIVEN `covered employees on the day notice is given` IS A NUMBER @desc How many covered employees you had on the day the notice was given
      `notices needing a report` IS A NUMBER @desc How many notices of retrenchment you gave that need a report
      `notices reported in time, in form and with every particular` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of those you reported in time, in the required form and with every particular
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, have I complied with retrenchment reporting` IF
    `the employer has complied with section 96A(2)` (`the section 96A facts under the 2019 Notification` `covered employees on the day notice is given` `notices needing a report` `notices reported in time, in form and with every particular`)

§§ `What I can be penalised for`

-- ss 126A(aa), 126B(1)(aa). Each retrenched employee not properly reported is
-- one occasion for an administrative penalty of the prescribed amount; the
-- officer "may" issue a notice, so this is the most the penalties could total.
-- The prescribed amount is not in the Act and is supplied.
@export What is the most my administrative penalties for retrenchment reporting could total, in dollars (ss 126A, 126B)?
GIVEN `covered employees on the day notice is given` IS A NUMBER @desc How many covered employees you had on the day the notice was given
      `notices needing a report` IS A NUMBER @desc How many notices of retrenchment you gave that need a report
      `notices reported in time, in form and with every particular` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of those you reported in time, in the required form and with every particular
      `the prescribed amount per occasion` IS A NUMBER @desc The prescribed administrative penalty for each occasion, in dollars (set by regulations, not the Act)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `As the employer, what is the most my retrenchment reporting penalties could total` IS
    `the most the administrative penalties could total` (`the section 96A facts under the 2019 Notification` `covered employees on the day notice is given` `notices needing a report` `notices reported in time, in form and with every particular`) `the prescribed amount per occasion`

-- s 126D(2). Failing, without reasonable excuse, to comply with an authorised
-- officer's direction to end or remedy a civil contravention is an offence.
@export Have I committed the offence of not complying with a direction (s 126D(2))?
GIVEN `a direction was given under section 126D(1)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an authorised officer gave you a direction under s 126D(1)
      `the employer failed to comply with it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you failed to comply with it
      `the employer had a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had a reasonable excuse
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `As the employer, have I committed the section 126D(2) offence` IF
    `the employer has committed the section 126D(2) offence` `a direction was given under section 126D(1)` `the employer failed to comply with it` `the employer had a reasonable excuse`

-- s 126D(2): "a fine not exceeding $5,000 or ... imprisonment for a term not
-- exceeding 6 months or ... both".
@export What is the most I can be fined for not complying with a direction, in dollars (s 126D(2))?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `As the employer, what is the most I can be fined for disobeying a direction` IS
    `the maximum fine for disobeying a section 126D direction`

@export What is the longest I can be imprisoned for not complying with a direction, in months (s 126D(2))?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `As the employer, what is the longest I can be imprisoned for disobeying a direction` IS
    `the maximum imprisonment in months for disobeying a section 126D direction`
